{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Akaike Information Criterion.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Akaike Information Criterion.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"akaike information criterion akaike information criterion","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Akaike Information Criterion.md","fileName":"Akaike Information Criterion.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Akaike Information Criterion","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 18:44\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Regression Model Development]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Regression Model Development","analyzinghealthdatainrforsasusers.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Akaike Information Criterion.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Akaike Information Criterion.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"akaike information criterion akaike information criterion","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Akaike Information Criterion.md","fileName":"Akaike Information Criterion.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Akaike Information Criterion","heading":"Akaike Information Criterion","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The Akaike information criterion, or AIC, compares models by combining lack of fit with a penalty for the number of estimated parameters. Among models fit to the same outcome data, a smaller AIC indicates a preferable balance under the criterion.\n\nAIC is relative: it has no universal good or perfect value. Unlike a likelihood-ratio comparison, it can compare some non-nested models, but it does not establish causal validity or repair a poorly defined outcome, exposure, or sample.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Regression Model Development","analyzinghealthdatainrforsasusers.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Akaike Information Criterion.md::2","sou